7. Enforcement Points

Atlas defines canonical enforcement points.

Enforcement Point	Description
Object Enforcement	Canonical Atlas Objects
Workflow Enforcement	Workflow execution
Security Enforcement	Authentication and authorization
Lifecycle Enforcement	Lifecycle transitions
Communication Enforcement	Message exchange
Validation Enforcement	Validation execution
Integration Enforcement	External systems
Runtime Enforcement	Platform behavior

Additional enforcement points MAY be introduced through future specifications.

8. Enforcement Actions

Atlas defines the following canonical enforcement actions.

Action	Description
Allow	Permit execution
Block	Prevent execution
Require Approval	Pause until approval
Notify	Inform participants
Escalate	Transfer to higher authority
Transform	Apply approved transformation
Start Workflow	Initiate workflow
Stop Workflow	Halt workflow
Record Audit	Create audit entry

Actions SHALL directly correspond to Canonical Policy Decisions.

9. Directive Processing

Each Policy Directive SHALL define:

Directive identifier
Source Policy Decision
Enforcement target
Required action
Execution constraints
Success criteria
Failure handling

Directives SHALL remain immutable after issuance.

10. Obligation Execution

Generated obligations SHALL support:

Assignment
Tracking
Progress monitoring
Completion verification
Escalation
Closure

Obligation execution SHALL remain independently auditable.

11. Runtime Monitoring

Every enforcement process SHALL expose:

Current status
Active directives
Pending obligations
Execution history
Failures
Performance metrics
Audit references

Runtime monitoring SHALL NOT modify enforcement behavior.

12. Exception Handling

Enforcement exceptions SHALL define:

Exception identifier
Triggering directive
Failure reason
Recovery strategy
Responsible authority
Resolution status

Exceptions SHALL generate Canonical Events.

13. Enforcement Lifecycle

Every enforcement SHALL support the following lifecycle.

Prepared
    │
Scheduled
    │
Executing
    │
Completed
    │
Verified
    │
Closed

Failed enforcement SHALL transition into managed exception handling.

14. Enforcement Governance

Every enforcement SHALL define:

Enforcement authority
Responsible participant
Applicable policies
Applicable directives
Audit requirements
Monitoring requirements

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout enforcement.

15. Enforcement Quality

Every enforcement implementation SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Determinism
Traceability
Explainability
Reliability
Recoverability
Auditability
Policy fidelity

Quality SHALL be measurable.

16. Conformance

A policy enforcement implementation conforms if it:

Consumes Canonical Policy Decisions.
Executes Canonical Policy Directives.
Preserves policy intent.
Records enforcement history.
Supports runtime monitoring.
Implements governed exception handling.
Maintains complete traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Autonomous enforcement agents.
Distributed enforcement coordination.
Adaptive enforcement strategies.
Self-healing enforcement mechanisms.
Predictive enforcement optimization.
Federated enforcement orchestration.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical enforcement semantics.

